{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794847838581,"sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","part":{"partID":"prt_1e5448d47002xRNm5ts3T3ZjOb","sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","messageID":"msg_1e5447d310017DyZsKFyHq0PEx","type":"tool","id":"call_51f8b494fcc74a2b9040104b","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '870,910p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts; echo \"=== typed exception ===\"; sed -n '1200,1250p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts; echo \"=== command-attach 340-380 ===\"; sed -n '340,380p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts"},"output":"// stand-in used under test reports \"shadow:\". Only a real cosine may carry a\n// command on meaning, because the stand-in measures shared words rather than\n// shared meaning.\nfunction commandMeaningCosine(contribution: LaneContribution | undefined): number | null {\n  if (!contribution) return null;\n  return contribution.evidence.some((entry) => entry.startsWith('cosine:'))\n    ? contribution.rawScore\n    : null;\n}\n\n// A command bridge passes on its own wording: one of its authored phrases or its\n// runnable slash string, which the explicit-author lane reports. A bridge that\n// passed on shared single tokens alone stays scored but stops passing, because\n// its phrases are everyday words and a command must not be offered for a prompt\n// that merely shares some of them. A paraphrase shares no words at all, so the\n// second way in is meaning: a real prompt embedding that clears both bars above.\n// Meaning-passed ids are collected so a later ordering step can tell them apart,\n// and a doctor problem in other words reaches /doctor:helper through\n// applyDoctorHelperHandoff instead.\n// A question that only wants an answer must not be handed a command that\n// changes things, however close the meaning. Phrase matches are already held to\n// this by the read-only confidence floor, and a meaning match lifts confidence\n// past that floor, so it has to ask the same question itself. A command whose\n// route only reads stays eligible for a plain explainer, because running it is\n// the answer. An explicit no-edit request rules out every command.\nexport function commandMeaningEligible(\n  promptLower: string,\n  readOnlyExplainer: boolean,\n  skill: SkillProjection | undefined,\n): boolean {\n  if (isNoEditRequest(promptLower)) return false;\n  if (!readOnlyExplainer) return true;\n  return skill !== undefined && skill.kind === 'command' && 'readOnly' in skill && skill.readOnly === true;\n}\n\nexport function requireCommandPhraseAnchor(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  confidenceThreshold: number = DEFAULT_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD,\n  meaningPassed: Set<string> = new Set(),\n  meaningEligible: (skillId: string) => boolean = () => true,\n  uncertaintyThreshold: number = DEFAULT_UNCERTAINTY_THRESHOLD,\n=== typed exception ===\n// A command row passes on its typed name, so a prompt that names the command to\n// refuse it or ask about it would otherwise pass that row. The skill behind the\n// command keeps its own pass, because refusing the command is not refusing the\n// skill.\nexport function withholdMentionedCommands(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  prompt: string,\n): AdvisorScoredRecommendation[] {\n  return ranked.map((recommendation) => {\n    if (recommendation.kind !== 'command' || !recommendation.passes_threshold) return recommendation;\n    // A row names a command with its subcommands, while a prompt names only the\n    // base, so both sides are compared on the base alone.\n    const command = commandRowFor(recommendation.skill)?.command.split(' ')[0];\n    if (command === undefined || !commandOnlyMentioned(prompt, command)) return recommendation;\n    return { ...recommendation, passes_threshold: false };\n  });\n}\n\n// A reader who ruled out a change must not be handed a command that writes,\n// whichever path chose it. A command that only reads is still the answer.\nexport function withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  promptLower: string,\n): AdvisorScoredRecommendation[] {\n  if (!isNoEditRequest(promptLower)) return [...ranked];\n  return ranked.map((recommendation) => {\n    if (recommendation.kind !== 'command' || !recommendation.passes_threshold) return recommendation;\n    if (commandRowFor(recommendation.skill)?.mutating === 'read-only') return recommendation;\n    // A command the reader typed and asked for is the request, and the no-edit words limit its scope.\n    const base = commandRowFor(recommendation.skill)?.command.split(' ')[0];\n    if (base !== undefined\n      && typedCommandIn(promptLower) === base\n      && !commandOnlyMentioned(promptLower, base)) return recommendation;\n    return { ...recommendation, passes_threshold: false };\n  });\n}\n\n// A typed command with no scorer row of its own can only reach the reader through\n// its owner, so the owner must lead. With no row the scorer already orders the\n// list, and a command that does have a row competes on its own evidence.\nexport function rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  promptLower: string,\n): AdvisorScoredRecommendation[] {\n  const command = typedCommandIn(promptLower);\n  if (command === undefined || !Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command)) return [...ranked];\n  // A command the reader refused must not pull its owner to the front, because\n  // the ranking would then lead with the skill that offers it.\n  if (commandOnlyMentioned(promptLower, command)) return [...ranked];\n  const row = TRACKED_COMMANDS[command];\n  if (row.scorerRow) return [...ranked];\n=== command-attach 340-380 ===\n// place, so a correction request names an edit to it rather than a new one. Every\n// other creation command names a purpose that a correction word belongs to.\nconst SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(['/create:readme', '/create:feature-catalog', '/create:manual-testing-playbook']);\n\n// Wording that asks for an answer and rules out a change. One pattern serves\n// every gate that has to honour it, so a new gate cannot drift from the first.\n// Words alone cannot tell \"make no changes to the files\" from a change request\n// that says what to leave alone, so both read as no change: losing an offer\n// costs less than offering a mutating command on a prompt that ruled one out.\n// A negated write verb counts only with an object that names nothing in\n// particular, because \"don't add tools\" limits a change and does not refuse it.\nexport const NO_EDIT_REQUEST = /\\b(no edits?|make no changes|no changes yet|without making changes|(?:do not|don't|dont) (?:make (?:any )?changes|change|edit|modify|touch|(?:create|write|add|delete|remove) (?:it|this|that|anything|any files?))|read-only only|no edits yet|only; do not|just show|just list)\\b/;\n\n// Many keyboards type the contraction with a curly apostrophe, so the text is\n// folded to the straight form before the pattern reads it.\nexport function isNoEditRequest(text: string): boolean {\n  return NO_EDIT_REQUEST.test(text.toLowerCase().replace(/[\\u2018\\u2019]/g, \"'\"));\n}\n\n// A refusal or a question reaches its command through a use verb, so the two\n// lists below share this one. A gap holds one or two plain words and no\n// punctuation, because a comma ends the clause that holds the lead.\nexport const COMMAND_USE_VERB = '(?:run|start|use|invoke|launch|execute|call|trigger|open|rerun|re-run|kick off)';\nexport const COMMAND_GAP_WORDS = \"(?:[a-z']+ ){0,2}\";\n\n// `why not` proposes the command rather than refusing it, so the word refuses\n// only where no `why` stands before it.\nconst COMMAND_NOT_WORD = '(?<!\\\\bwhy )not';\n\n// A command the reader refuses is named without being asked for, so this list\n// holds the leads that mark that naming. A lead must sit directly before the\n// command so a later clause cannot refuse an earlier request, and a gap holds\n// plain words only so a refusal before a comma cannot swallow the request after\n// it. A reader who stops using a command names it only to refuse it.\nexport const COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS: readonly string[] = [\n  `(?:do not|don't|dont|never|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}|won't|wont|no longer|cannot|can't|no need to|need not|needn't) (?!(?:forget|hesitate|fail|mind)\\\\b)${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}${COMMAND_USE_VERB} ${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}`,\n  `(?:skip|skipping|without|instead of|rather than|avoid|stop|stopped|quit|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}) (?:(?!${COMMAND_USE_VERB}\\\\b)[a-z']+ ){0,2}`,\n];\n\n// A refusal or a question over an or-list covers every command the list joins,\n// while a second use verb opens a clause of its own, so the list joins only `or`\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"// stand-in used under test reports \"shadow:\". Only a real cosine may carry a\n// command on meaning, because the stand-in measures shared words rather than\n// shared meaning.\nfunction commandMeaningCosine(contribution: LaneContribution | undefined): number | null {\n  if (!contribution) return null;\n  return contribution.evidence.some((entry) => entry.startsWith('cosine:'))\n    ? contribution.rawScore\n    : null;\n}\n\n// A command bridge passes on its own wording: one of its authored phrases or its\n// runnable slash string, which the explicit-author lane reports. A bridge that\n// passed on shared single tokens alone stays scored but stops passing, because\n// its phrases are everyday words and a command must not be offered for a prompt\n// that merely shares some of them. A paraphrase shares no words at all, so the\n// second way in is meaning: a real prompt embedding that clears both bars above.\n// Meaning-passed ids are collected so a later ordering step can tell them apart,\n// and a doctor problem in other words reaches /doctor:helper through\n// applyDoctorHelperHandoff instead.\n// A question that only wants an answer must not be handed a command that\n// changes things, however close the meaning. Phrase matches are already held to\n// this by the read-only confidence floor, and a meaning match lifts confidence\n// past that floor, so it has to ask the same question itself. A command whose\n// route only reads stays eligible for a plain explainer, because running it is\n// the answer. An explicit no-edit request rules out every command.\nexport function commandMeaningEligible(\n  promptLower: string,\n  readOnlyExplainer: boolean,\n  skill: SkillProjection | undefined,\n): boolean {\n  if (isNoEditRequest(promptLower)) return false;\n  if (!readOnlyExplainer) return true;\n  return skill !== undefined && skill.kind === 'command' && 'readOnly' in skill && skill.readOnly === true;\n}\n\nexport function requireCommandPhraseAnchor(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  confidenceThreshold: number = DEFAULT_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD,\n  meaningPassed: Set<string> = new Set(),\n  meaningEligible: (skillId: string) => boolean = () => true,\n  uncertaintyThreshold: number = DEFAULT_UNCERTAINTY_THRESHOLD,\n=== typed exception ===\n// A command row passes on its typed name, so a prompt that names the command to\n// refuse it or ask about it would otherwise pass that row. The skill behind the\n// command keeps its own pass, because refusing the command is not refusing the\n// skill.\nexport function withholdMentionedCommands(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  prompt: string,\n): AdvisorScoredRecommendation[] {\n  return ranked.map((recommendation) => {\n    if (recommendation.kind !== 'command' || !recommendation.passes_threshold) return recommendation;\n    // A row names a command with its subcommands, while a prompt names only the\n    // base, so both sides are compared on the base alone.\n    const command = commandRowFor(recommendation.skill)?.command.split(' ')[0];\n    if (command === undefined || !commandOnlyMentioned(prompt, command)) return recommendation;\n    return { ...recommendation, passes_threshold: false };\n  });\n}\n\n// A reader who ruled out a change must not be handed a command that writes,\n// whichever path chose it. A command that only reads is still the answer.\nexport function withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  promptLower: string,\n): AdvisorScoredRecommendation[] {\n  if (!isNoEditRequest(promptLower)) return [...ranked];\n  return ranked.map((recommendation) => {\n    if (recommendation.kind !== 'command' || !recommendation.passes_threshold) return recommendation;\n    if (commandRowFor(recommendation.skill)?.mutating === 'read-only') return recommendation;\n    // A command the reader typed and asked for is the request, and the no-edit words limit its scope.\n    const base = commandRowFor(recommendation.skill)?.command.split(' ')[0];\n    if (base !== undefined\n      && typedCommandIn(promptLower) === base\n      && !commandOnlyMentioned(promptLower, base)) return recommendation;\n    return { ...recommendation, passes_threshold: false };\n  });\n}\n\n// A typed command with no scorer row of its own can only reach the reader through\n// its owner, so the owner must lead. With no row the scorer already orders the\n// list, and a command that does have a row competes on its own evidence.\nexport function rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(\n  ranked: readonly AdvisorScoredRecommendation[],\n  promptLower: string,\n): AdvisorScoredRecommendation[] {\n  const command = typedCommandIn(promptLower);\n  if (command === undefined || !Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command)) return [...ranked];\n  // A command the reader refused must not pull its owner to the front, because\n  // the ranking would then lead with the skill that offers it.\n  if (commandOnlyMentioned(promptLower, command)) return [...ranked];\n  const row = TRACKED_COMMANDS[command];\n  if (row.scorerRow) return [...ranked];\n=== command-attach 340-380 ===\n// place, so a correction request names an edit to it rather than a new one. Every\n// other creation command names a purpose that a correction word belongs to.\nconst SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(['/create:readme', '/create:feature-catalog', '/create:manual-testing-playbook']);\n\n// Wording that asks for an answer and rules out a change. One pattern serves\n// every gate that has to honour it, so a new gate cannot drift from the first.\n// Words alone cannot tell \"make no changes to the files\" from a change request\n// that says what to leave alone, so both read as no change: losing an offer\n// costs less than offering a mutating command on a prompt that ruled one out.\n// A negated write verb counts only with an object that names nothing in\n// particular, because \"don't add tools\" limits a change and does not refuse it.\nexport const NO_EDIT_REQUEST = /\\b(no edits?|make no changes|no changes yet|without making changes|(?:do not|don't|dont) (?:make (?:any )?changes|change|edit|modify|touch|(?:create|write|add|delete|remove) (?:it|this|that|anything|any files?))|read-only only|no edits yet|only; do not|just show|just list)\\b/;\n\n// Many keyboards type the contraction with a curly apostrophe, so the text is\n// folded to the straight form before the pattern reads it.\nexport function isNoEditRequest(text: string): boolean {\n  return NO_EDIT_REQUEST.test(text.toLowerCase().replace(/[\\u2018\\u2019]/g, \"'\"));\n}\n\n// A refusal or a question reaches its command through a use verb, so the two\n// lists below share this one. A gap holds one or two plain words and no\n// punctuation, because a comma ends the clause that holds the lead.\nexport const COMMAND_USE_VERB = '(?:run|start|use|invoke|launch|execute|call|trigger|open|rerun|re-run|kick off)';\nexport const COMMAND_GAP_WORDS = \"(?:[a-z']+ ){0,2}\";\n\n// `why not` proposes the command rather than refusing it, so the word refuses\n// only where no `why` stands before it.\nconst COMMAND_NOT_WORD = '(?<!\\\\bwhy )not';\n\n// A command the reader refuses is named without being asked for, so this list\n// holds the leads that mark that naming. A lead must sit directly before the\n// command so a later clause cannot refuse an earlier request, and a gap holds\n// plain words only so a refusal before a comma cannot swallow the request after\n// it. A reader who stops using a command names it only to refuse it.\nexport const COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS: readonly string[] = [\n  `(?:do not|don't|dont|never|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}|won't|wont|no longer|cannot|can't|no need to|need not|needn't) (?!(?:forget|hesitate|fail|mind)\\\\b)${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}${COMMAND_USE_VERB} ${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}`,\n  `(?:skip|skipping|without|instead of|rather than|avoid|stop|stopped|quit|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}) (?:(?!${COMMAND_USE_VERB}\\\\b)[a-z']+ ){0,2}`,\n];\n\n// A refusal or a question over an or-list covers every command the list joins,\n// while a second use verb opens a clause of its own, so the list joins only `or`\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '870,910p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts; echo \\\"=== typed exception ===\\\"; sed -n '1200,1250p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts; echo \\\"=== command-attach 340-380 ===\\\"; sed -n '340,380p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794847837511,"end":1794847838581}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794847840407,"sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","part":{"id":"prt_1e5449897001vOyZXi3hY9HHXg","sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","messageID":"msg_1e5447d310017DyZsKFyHq0PEx","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"4577ee14f56999b83b9d8a26c56e858c499f0ee9","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1637,"output":169,"reasoning":143,"cache":{"read":126848,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794847845012,"sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","part":{"id":"prt_1e544aa94001lz1ylDaFNtB4jW","sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","messageID":"msg_1e544989f001wZg9y0XvRGENRV","type":"step-start","snapshot":"4577ee14f56999b83b9d8a26c56e858c499f0ee9"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794847853750,"sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","part":{"partID":"prt_1e544cab8001T20Z9DjZo3jzVs","sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e70ffe7efVZgFVdgfHx6","messageID":"msg_1e544989f001wZg9y0XvRGENRV","type":"tool","id":"call_bfd06199b03842f382ac384e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '30,75p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md"},"output":"Some briefs also offered the wrong command. A hub could offer a writing command to a prompt that asked for no edits. A mention of `/deep:review` opened the gate for `/deep:research`. A file name could read as a typed command.\n\nThis release also stops offering a command where none fits the request. It adds a measure of which workflow a run opened.\n\n&nbsp;\n\n## What's New at a Glance\n\n- **Every prompt writes a health record.** It carries no prompt, skill label or error text. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n- **`hookHealth` shows answers from the local scorer.** It also gives the time of the newest record. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n- **A hub says `(needs a mode)` only when you named it.** A hub the scorer guessed shows no label. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **The top hub keeps its command in the hook.** A row the brief never shows can no longer take it. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **A tool that does not show up reaches the doctor helper.** The brief names `/doctor:helper` beside the skills. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **A bare problem word can now lead with a doctor command.** It needs a skill that matched on shared words only. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **A no-edit prompt gets no command it did not type.** More wordings now count as no-edit. <!-- section: No-Edit Prompts -->\n- **A refusal or a question is not a request.** A command the prompt refuses or asks about is not offered. A command named with no such lead is, when its owner and route gates pass. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n- **A refusal covers a whole or-list.** `no need to run` counts, and a question mark ends the sentence before it. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n- **Every runtime leads with the same head.** The head row keeps its command under every request shape. <!-- section: One Head on Every Runtime -->\n- **The summary says which workflow a run opened.** It sorts each run into one path, such as a command document or the owning skill. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n- **A load now counts in more shapes.** A shell read and a prompt mirror each count, and a search does not. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n- **The create-skill command row has a new id.** It is now `command-create-sk-skill`. <!-- section: Renamed Command -->\n- **The OpenCode brief drops a repeated route line.** The head already names the hub and its command. <!-- section: OpenCode Plugin -->\n\n&nbsp;\n\n## Hook Health\n\n#### A Record on Every Prompt\n\nThe Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi, DSH and Vibe hooks share one handler. That handler now appends a health record on every prompt. Before, it wrote one only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set. A record holds the runtime, the status, the freshness, the duration and the cache hit. A disabled hook writes one too:\n\n```json\n{\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-13T20:30:20.999Z\",\"runtime\":\"claude\",\"status\":\"skipped\",\"freshness\":\"unavailable\",\"durationMs\":1,\"cacheHit\":false}\n```\n\n`SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` now only adds the skill label and the error text. The log trims back to the newest 200 records once it passes 300. The OpenCode plugin writes no record.\n\n#### Health Shows Degraded Answers\n\n`advisor_status` carries `hookHealth` once any hook has written a record. It still summarizes the newest 30 records. Two fields are new. `rollingDegradedRate` is the share of answers from the CLI's local scorer. `lastRecordAt` is the time of the newest record. A runtime that stopped writing now shows its age.\n\n#### The Direct Call Reads the Switch\n\nA direct `advisor_recommend` call now abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off. It decides in the caller's own process, under `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases, the master switch or the flags file. That happens before any daemon starts or the scorer runs. The launcher passes no switch name to a daemon it starts.\n\n#### Smaller Fixes\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Some briefs also offered the wrong command. A hub could offer a writing command to a prompt that asked for no edits. A mention of `/deep:review` opened the gate for `/deep:research`. A file name could read as a typed command.\n\nThis release also stops offering a command where none fits the request. It adds a measure of which workflow a run opened.\n\n&nbsp;\n\n## What's New at a Glance\n\n- **Every prompt writes a health record.** It carries no prompt, skill label or error text. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n- **`hookHealth` shows answers from the local scorer.** It also gives the time of the newest record. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n- **A hub says `(needs a mode)` only when you named it.** A hub the scorer guessed shows no label. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **The top hub keeps its command in the hook.** A row the brief never shows can no longer take it. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **A tool that does not show up reaches the doctor helper.** The brief names `/doctor:helper` beside the skills. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **A bare problem word can now lead with a doctor command.** It needs a skill that matched on shared words only. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n- **A no-edit prompt gets no command it did not type.** More wordings now count as no-edit. <!-- section: No-Edit Prompts -->\n- **A refusal or a question is not a request.** A command the prompt refuses or asks about is not offered. A command named with no such lead is, when its owner and route gates pass. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n- **A refusal covers a whole or-list.** `no need to run` counts, and a question mark ends the sentence before it. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n- **Every runtime leads with the same head.** The head row keeps its command under every request shape. <!-- section: One Head on Every Runtime -->\n- **The summary says which workflow a run opened.** It sorts each run into one path, such as a command document or the owning skill. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n- **A load now counts in more shapes.** A shell read and a prompt mirror each count, and a search does not. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n- **The create-skill command row has a new id.** It is now `command-create-sk-skill`. <!-- section: Renamed Command -->\n- **The OpenCode brief drops a repeated route line.** The head already names the hub and its command. <!-- section: OpenCode Plugin -->\n\n&nbsp;\n\n## Hook Health\n\n#### A Record on Every Prompt\n\nThe Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi, DSH and Vibe hooks share one handler. That handler now appends a health record on every prompt. Before, it wrote one only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set. A record holds the runtime, the status, the freshness, the duration and the cache hit. A disabled hook writes one too:\n\n```json\n{\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-13T20:30:20.999Z\",\"runtime\":\"claude\",\"status\":\"skipped\",\"freshness\":\"unavailable\",\"durationMs\":1,\"cacheHit\":false}\n```\n\n`SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` now only adds the skill label and the error text. The log trims back to the newest 200 records once it passes 300. The OpenCode plugin writes no record.\n\n#### Health Shows Degraded Answers\n\n`advisor_status` carries `hookHealth` once any hook has written a record. It still summarizes the newest 30 records. Two fields are new. `rollingDegradedRate` is the share of answers from the CLI's local scorer. `lastRecordAt` is the time of the newest record. A runtime that stopped writing now shows its age.\n\n#### The Direct Call Reads the Switch\n\nA direct `advisor_recommend` call now abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off. It decides in the caller's own process, under `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases, the master switch or the flags file. That happens before any daemon starts or the scorer runs. The launcher passes no switch name to a daemon it starts.\n\n#### Smaller Fixes\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '30,75p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794847853240,"end":1794847853750}}}}
